Subject: [PATCH] Scene-scoped binding resolution (editor cost + cross-scene fallback)

A binding anchored in a scene that is not loaded can never resolve.
ResolveBinding called GlobalObjectIdentifierToObjectSlow anyway, which is
slow AND fires a native "Assertion failed: manager != NULL", dragging a
managed stack-trace extraction and a log write behind it. OnHierarchyChanged
retried every such binding on EVERY hierarchy change, so in a multi-scene
show (one content scene loaded at a time, preset recalls churning the
hierarchy) this was paid dozens of times per recall. Measured: 868
assertions in one editor session, 100% of them via OnHierarchyChanged.

 - guard ResolveBinding on the anchor's scene being loaded (fails open:
   non-scene anchors and undeterminable scenes keep the old path);
 - gate the re-resolution pass in OnHierarchyChanged on the loaded-scene
   set actually changing, and subscribe the events that can change the
   outcome (sceneOpened/Closed, sceneLoaded/Unloaded, playModeStateChanged)
   with a one-frame deferred retry, so resolution timing does not regress.

Public surface unchanged. Unresolved state identical to before.
Package: com.blindspot.kontrols 0.5.1 - Unity 6000.3.10f1
Rationale, decisions and measurements: see RAPPORT.md shipped alongside.
